<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad III as-Sadiq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sadok Bey, Muhammad as-Sadiq Bey · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad III as-Sadiq was bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a> from 1859 to 1882. In 1861 he promulgated a constitution, the first in the Arab world, but it was suspended in 1864 after a revolt against higher taxes. Heavy debts led to an international financial commission in 1869, and after a French invasion in 1881 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a>, accepting a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq.jpg" alt="Muhammad III as-Sadiq" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Simil (1822-?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sadok_Bey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad as-Sadiq, commonly known as Sadok Bey, was born on 7 February 1813. He was invested as Bey al-Mahalla, the heir apparent, on 10 June 1855, the same day he was named a divisional general in the Ottoman army, and he succeeded his brother Muhammad II ibn al-Husayn on 23 September 1859. On 10 December 1859 he was promoted to the rank of marshal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In July 1860, persuaded by the British consul Richard Wood, the bey allowed a British subject named Holt to set up the country's first official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press and its first Arabic-language newspaper, ar-Ra'id at-Tūnisi. The constitution of 23 April 1861 divided executive, legislative and judicial power among a Supreme Council, a legislature and new courts, limited the bey's powers and gave Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> equal rights, above all in owning property, which drew French traders and religious schools to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Three days later he changed the succession, so that the oldest prince of the family would inherit rather than the late ruler's eldest son.</p><p>He had the Marseille engineer Colin repair the Zaghouan aqueduct that supplied the capital, and in 1865 began demolishing the walls of the medina, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> lost gates such as Bab Cartagena, Bab Souika, Bab Bnet and Bab El Jazira. Meanwhile intrigues among his ministers, notably Mustapha Khaznadar and Mustapha Ben Ismaïl, the pressure of the European consuls and the approaching bankruptcy of the state brought on the Mejba Revolt of 1864 and debts he could not repay, despite the efforts of his Grand Vizier Kheireddine Pacha.</p><p>In April 1881 raids by Khroumire tribesmen across the border of French Algeria gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> its pretext. A French expeditionary force took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicca-veneria">El Kef</a>, troops landed at Bizerte on 1 May, and the French army occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> on 11 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His main residence was the Ksar Said palace, built in an Italianate style in front of the Bardo palace, and it was there, on 12 May 1881, that the French consul Théodore Roustan brought General Jules Aimé Bréart to obtain his signature on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a>. In his later years the bey fell under the influence of Mustapha Ben Ismaïl. He died on 27 October 1882 without children, was buried in the Tourbet el Bey mausoleum in the medina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and was succeeded by his brother Ali III ibn al-Husayn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad III as-Sadiq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Using border raids as a pretext, France invaded the Beylik of Tunis in 1881 and forced <b>Muhammad as-Sadiq Bey</b> to sign the Treaty of Bardo.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tunisia">French protectorate of Tunisia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Le Bardo, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Le Bardo, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.81,10.14&amp;year=1857" aria-label="Le Bardo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad III as-Sadiq"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 4388h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 4388h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.81,10.14&amp;year=1857">What was here in 1857? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
